meta {
  name: 06 Upsert Result Values
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/lab-reports/{{labReportId}}/results
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "values": [
      {
        "resultParamId": "{{resultParamId}}",
        "observed1": "1.1",
        "observed2": "",
        "unit": "mg/dL",
        "methodology": "Enzymatic",
        "referenceRangeId": "{{referenceRangeId}}",
        "referenceDisplay": "0.6 - 1.3 mg/dL"
      }
    ]
  }
}

docs {
  Bulk upsert result values on the Test Entry grid (LABORATORY.docx §4.3).
  Does NOT change the report's status — call Save or Submit afterward. One
  row per `resultParamId`; re-sending the same id updates it in place.

  Dynamic/calculated parameters (`parameterType = CALCULATED` on the test's
  result params) are re-evaluated authoritatively from their formula AFTER this
  upsert, over the report's current values, and stored with `source = CALCULATED`
  (dependency chains evaluate in order). To keep a manual override on a
  calculated parameter, send it with `"isManualOverride": true` — it is then
  stored with `source = MANUAL` and left out of the recompute until reset.
  A formula that can't be computed yet (blank/non-numeric input, division by
  zero) blanks that slot rather than failing the save.

  **Analyzer (machine) values.** A value an analyzer filled (`source = ADAPTER`,
  `enteredBy` = the analyzer's id) ALWAYS stays the analyzer's record: a Save never
  changes its source, analyzer id or the time the analyzer sent it, whoever edits it.
  When Observed 1 and Observed 2 are unchanged (trimmed; empty equals null; the unit
  is not compared) only the range id, display text and method are updated (the unit
  only when the analyzer sent none). A changed number, or a range / unit change the
  technician confirmed (`overridesAnalyzer`), is an overwrite: the new number, unit
  and range are stored and the change is recorded in the report's Audit Trail
  (`GET /lab-reports/:id/history`, action `ANALYZER_VALUE_OVERWRITTEN`) with the
  analyzer's name and when it sent the value: "Value edited — <param>: <old> → <new>"
  for a number, "Range changed — <param>: <old range> → <new range>; result <value>
  kept" when only the range or unit changed (the analyzer's number is never cleared
  or converted by a range change), "Value and range edited" for both. The same
  confirmed change sent twice writes one entry.
  - `overridesAnalyzer` (optional boolean): the technician confirmed overwriting the
    analyzer's value or changing its range. It only matters when the number changed or
    the range / unit really differs from what is stored; sent on its own with nothing
    different it is not an overwrite and nothing is recorded.
  - `loadedUpdatedAt` (optional ISO date): the value's `updatedAt` as the screen loaded
    it. If the stored analyzer value has changed since (the analyzer re-sent a result
    while the screen was open) the whole save is refused with 409
    `LAB_REPORT_VALUE_CHANGED` and nothing is written; reload and review. Omit it to
    skip the check.
  Both fields are optional; older clients that omit them keep working. Unknown
  fields are rejected (400), so deploy the backend before a frontend that sends them.

  **Default / Highlight / Not Highlight (all three result types).** The Test Entry
  grid lets the technician choose, per parameter, how its observed value(s) print:
  - `flagOption` (optional enum `ResultFlagOption`): `DEFAULT` — the system decides (a
    Quantitative number outside its range prints in the parameter's Abnormal Flag Logic
    style, with the word High/Low if that is a "…with High and Low" style; everything
    else prints Normal; a parameter with NO style set prints Normal too); `HIGHLIGHT` — print in `highlightLogic` whatever the value;
    `NOT_HIGHLIGHT` — always Normal. Omitted = the stored choice is left as it is (a new
    row starts on `DEFAULT`). Applies to Observed 1 and Observed 2 alike.
  - `highlightLogic` (optional enum `AbnormalFlag`): the style under `HIGHLIGHT` — one of
    the nine BASIC styles; a "…with High and Low" style is refused (422
    `VALIDATION_ERROR`). Omitted under `HIGHLIGHT` = the basic style of the parameter's
    own style, or Bold and Red when the parameter has no style. Stored as null under `DEFAULT` / `NOT_HIGHLIGHT`.
  - A user WITHOUT the `lab_operations:reporting__change_abnormal_style` permission
    ("Change abnormal style") may only send, or omit, the style derived from the
    parameter's own style; any other `highlightLogic` is refused with 403
    `ABNORMAL_STYLE_CHANGE_NOT_ALLOWED` and nothing is written. The choice is saved for
    an analyzer's value too (it is the technician's, not part of the analyzer's record).
    A `null` or omitted `highlightLogic` under `HIGHLIGHT` means "the parameter's own
    style" (Bold and Red when the parameter has none) and never needs the permission. A Re-run, or a return of the sample, resets the
    choice to `DEFAULT` (the soft-deleted row is revived by the next save or analyzer value).
  The retired `highlightInReport` boolean is no longer accepted (400).

  404 `LAB_REPORT_NOT_FOUND`. 409 `LAB_REPORT_LOCKED` if the report is locked.
  409 `LAB_REPORT_VALUE_CHANGED` if an analyzer value changed since `loadedUpdatedAt`.
}
